{"id":799,"date":"2020-11-05T12:59:46","date_gmt":"2020-11-05T20:59:46","guid":{"rendered":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/?post_type=lesson&#038;p=799"},"modified":"2023-08-21T08:41:34","modified_gmt":"2023-08-21T15:41:34","slug":"timers","status":"publish","type":"lesson","link":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/timers\/","title":{"rendered":"3E: Timers"},"content":{"rendered":"<div class=\"wpb-content-wrapper\"><p>[vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221667dbb33\" name=\"6a221667dbb33\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 1<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>How does the timer work?<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Timers are everywhere so they might not seem like a big deal, but being able to use a timer in your program is an incredibly useful and powerful skill! This can help you keep better track of your flights and the movements within them.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">With CoDrone Mini, time is stored in variables. For this lesson, you will be storing the start time in one variable, the end time in another, and then subtracting the two to get the total elapsed time.<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221667dbc69\" name=\"6a221667dbc69\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 2<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Total flight timer<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">For this activity, you will be programming a timer to keep track of your CoDrone Mini\u2019s total flight, starting at takeoff and finishing with landing.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After importing the CoDrone Mini and time libraries, creating the drone object, and pairing, create a variable to store the drone\u2019s starting time (we called ours \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">start_time<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">). Have your drone take off and land, and then create another variable to store the drone\u2019s ending time (we called ours \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">end_time<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">).<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">To get the total time of your drone\u2019s flight, create a variable that will subtract <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">start_time<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> from <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">end_time<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. We called ours \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">elapsed_time<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">. Finally, use a print command to have your program print the elapsed time in seconds.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Once you have tried this out, add hovering and then some flight movements into your program. What changes do you notice?<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your code should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\nstart_time = time.time()\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\nend_time = time.time()\r\n\r\n\r\nelapsed_time = end_time - start_time<\/pre>\n<p>&nbsp;<\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">print<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">(elapsed_time)<\/span>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221667dbced\" name=\"6a221667dbced\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 3<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Movement timer<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">What if you wanted to check how long one section in your program took, like how long your drone takes to fly in one square, or if pitching forward for 2 seconds actually takes 2 seconds? There\u2019s a way to do that, and it just involves moving some timers around in your program!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After importing the CoDrone Mini and time libraries, creating the drone object, and pairing, have your drone take off, and then set pitch to 40. Next, create a variable to store the drone\u2019s starting time, and then have your drone move for 2 seconds. It is very important to start the timer in this location! Starting it before setting pitch will add extra time, and starting it after the drone moves will skip over what you\u2019re trying to time.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After the drone\u2019s move command, create another variable to store the drone\u2019s ending time, and then have the drone land. Create another variable that will subtract \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">start_time<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">from \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">end_time<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">, and use a print command to have your program print the elapsed time in seconds.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After trying it out, use a timer and see if the move command actually takes the amount of time you put in the parameters. If there is extra time, where do you think it\u2019s coming from? Move the timers around, add more flight movement, and see what happens!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your code should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"python\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(40)\r\n\r\nstart_time = time.time()\r\n\r\ndrone.move(2)\r\n\r\nend_time = time.time()\r\n\r\ndrone.land()\r\n\r\n\r\nelapsed_time = end_time - start_time\r\n\r\nprint(elapsed_time)<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221667dbd69\" name=\"6a221667dbd69\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 4<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Using timers in a function<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Creating multiple variables to time one thing can be a lot. In cases when you want to time one movement, you can create a function instead! This will run a timer for the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">move()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">function for however many seconds you put in the parameters.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">After importing the CoDrone Mini and time libraries, creating the drone object, and pairing, define a function (we called ours \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">fly()<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">) and put <\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">seconds<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\"> in the parameters. Next, create a variable to store the drone\u2019s starting time. Create a while loop so that if the time minus the start time is less than the seconds in the parameters, the drone will move.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Next, have your drone take off and set the pitch to 30. Include the <code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">fly()<\/code><\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">\u00a0function and set the parameters to 2 seconds, and then have the drone land.<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Play around with the time in the parameters. What happens? Does increasing or decreasing the pitch have an impact on the time?<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Your code should look something like this:<\/span><\/p>\n<pre class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">import CoDrone_mini\r\n\r\nimport time\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone = CoDrone_mini.CoDrone()\r\n\r\ndrone.pair()\r\n\r\n\r\n# -------- function definition ---------\r\n\r\ndef fly(seconds):\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0start_time = time.time()\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0while time.time() - start_time &lt; seconds:\r\n\r\n\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0\u00a0drone.move()\r\n\r\n# --------------------------------------\r\n\r\n\r\ndrone.takeoff()\r\n\r\ndrone.set_pitch(30)\r\n\r\nfly(2)\r\n\r\ndrone.land()<\/pre>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row][vc_row][vc_column]<a id=\"6a221667dbddf\" name=\"6a221667dbddf\" class=\"scroll-spy-anchor\"><\/a>\n<div class=\"robolink-step \">\n\n\t\t<div class=\"robolink-step-head\"><span>Step 5<\/span><\/div>\n\t\n\t\t\t<h2>Challenge<\/h2>\n\t\n\t<p>[vc_column_text]<b>Challenge: One Minute Code<\/b><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Fit as much flight movement as you can into one minute exactly (according to the \u00a0<code class=\"EnlighterJSRAW\" data-enlighter-language=\"generic\">elapsed_time<\/code>\u00a0<\/span><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">variable in your program)!<\/span><\/p>\n<p><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Rules:<\/span><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">You must include at least one pitch, one throttle, one yaw, and one roll.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Any flight movement, including hovering or delaying, can only last for 3 seconds.<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">The same movement cannot be used in consecutive order, whether that is one after the other or in a loop (so don\u2019t loop pitching forward for 3 seconds 20 times).<\/span><\/li>\n<li style=\"font-weight: 400;\"><span style=\"font-weight: 400;\">Takeoff and landing are included in the one minute.<\/span><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>[\/vc_column_text]<\/p>\n<\/div>\n[\/vc_column][\/vc_row]<\/p>\n<\/div>","protected":false},"excerpt":{"rendered":"[vc_row][vc_column] Step 6 How does the timer work? [vc_column_text]Timers are everywhere so they might not seem like a big deal, but being able to use a timer in your program&nbsp;<a href=\"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/lesson\/timers\/\">&hellip;<\/a>","protected":false},"featured_media":1730,"template":"","meta":{"_initial_content":"","_new_post":false,"_quiz_has_questions":false,"_lesson_complexity":"","_lesson_length":10,"_lesson_course":744,"_lesson_preview":"preview"},"lesson-category":[1782,1848,1793,1826,1482,853,694,59,171,63],"lesson-tag":[2135,2125,2122,2134,2139],"class_list":["post-799","lesson","type-lesson","status-publish","has-post-thumbnail","hentry","lesson-tag-beginner","lesson-tag-codrone-mini","lesson-tag-programming","lesson-tag-python","lesson-tag-timers","module-beginner","post","odd"],"_links":{"self":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/799","targetHints":{"allow":["GET"]}}],"collection":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons"}],"about":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/types\/lesson"}],"version-history":[{"count":2,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/799\/revisions"}],"predecessor-version":[{"id":2791,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/lessons\/799\/revisions\/2791"}],"wp:featuredmedia":[{"embeddable":true,"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media\/1730"}],"wp:attachment":[{"href":"https:\/\/archive.learn.robolink.com\/wp-json\/wp\/v2\/media?parent=799"}],"wp:te